# The headless generation engine

The engine turns a collection of literate documents into a complete generation
plan. It does not read or write files, depend on VS Code, or consult platform
state. CLI and editor adapters supply the inputs and decide when to write a
successful result. This chapter is authoritative for `src/engine.ts`.

`generateProject(documents, templates = {})` returns generated files and
source-position diagnostics. Its central guarantee is atomic planning: **any
diagnostic makes the entire file list empty**, including documentation HTML.
A valid plan contains both tangled source and rendered documentation.

The root fragment assembles the implementation in dependency order. The final
fragment describes the generation phases; the preceding fragments provide
shared contracts and helpers for both generation and its adapters.

```ts : <<headless generation engine.*>>= ./src/engine.ts $
<<engine interfaces and dependencies>>
<<engine path and source validation>>
<<engine document ordering>>
<<engine template dependency discovery>>
<<engine literal template substitution>>
<<engine default HTML template>>
<<engine generation orchestration>>
```

## Contracts at the adapter boundary

A `SourceDocument` supplies a workspace-relative POSIX path and source text.
A `GeneratedFile` supplies a relative destination and its complete content.
Templates arrive as an explicit mapping of paths to text, rather than being
loaded implicitly. All mutable generation state belongs to a single call.

Diagnostic `line` and `column` coordinates are zero-based; columns count
JavaScript string positions. Adapters may translate them for display without
changing the engine contract. Internal fragment parts retain their own source
locations, so an error in an appended part points back to that part's document.
Markdown-it supplies parsing and rendering, while highlight.js handles known
code languages. Adapters share the readonly `discoveryExcludedDirectories`
list to avoid discovering sources in version control, dependency, build, and
test-runtime directories.

```ts : <<engine interfaces and dependencies>>=
import MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
import Token = require('markdown-it/lib/token');
import hljs from 'highlight.js';

export interface SourceDocument { path: string; text: string; }
export interface GenerationDiagnostic { path: string; line: number; column: number; message: string; }
export interface GeneratedFile { path: string; content: string; }

export const discoveryExcludedDirectories: readonly string[] = Object.freeze([
    '.git', 'node_modules', 'out', 'dist', '.yarn', '.vscode-test'
]);

interface Location { path: string; line: number; column: number; }
interface Header { name: string; language: string; append: boolean; output?: string; template?: string; }
interface Part { content: string; location: Location; }
interface Fragment { header: Header; parts: Part[]; location: Location; }

```

## Paths and source text must survive parsing unchanged

`safeRelativePath` canonicalizes paths inside the workspace, rejecting absolute
paths, backslashes, control characters, and parent traversal beyond the root.
The policy is portable even when generation runs on a case-sensitive host:
components cannot contain Windows-invalid characters (including colons used
for alternate data streams), end in a dot or space, or use reserved device
names such as `CON`, `NUL.txt`, or `COM1`. Device names are checked without
regard to case, including extensions and Windows' reserved superscript digits.
The navigation components `.` and `..` are handled first, so parent traversal
that remains within the workspace still normalizes safely.

Collision keys use locale-independent lowercase paths for sources, templates,
and outputs. Output/input aliases, duplicate output aliases, and ancestor
conflicts are rejected: a planned file cannot also be a directory containing
another planned file or supplied input. This conservative lexical policy does
not replace adapters' filesystem checks, including symbolic-link handling.

The shared header grammar distinguishes fragment definitions and appends from
their output filename and optional settings. Empty output names are rejected
later when collecting definitions. Actual NUL characters are rejected in source
text **before** Markdown-it can normalize them into replacement characters,
which would otherwise conceal invalid names. Their diagnostic coordinates are
computed from the original text, including its original line endings.

```ts : <<engine path and source validation>>=
/** Canonical workspace-relative POSIX path; never permits escaping the workspace. */
export function safeRelativePath(value: string): string | undefined {
    if (!value || /^(?:\/|[a-zA-Z]:)/.test(value) || /[\\\x00-\x1f]/.test(value)) { return undefined; }
    const parts: string[] = [];
    for (const part of value.split('/')) {
        if (!part || part === '.') { continue; }
        if (part === '..') {
            if (!parts.length) { return undefined; }
            parts.pop();
        } else {
            if (/[<>:"|?*]/.test(part) || /[. ]$/.test(part)) { return undefined; }
            const device = part.split('.')[0].replace(/ +$/, '');
            if (/^(?:con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9¹²³]|lpt[1-9¹²³]|conin\$|conout\$)$/i.test(device)) { return undefined; }
            parts.push(part);
        }
    }
    return parts.length ? parts.join('/') : undefined;
}

/** Paths are already normalized before computing portable collision keys. */
function pathKey(path: string): string { return path.toLowerCase(); }

function ancestorConflict(left: string, right: string): boolean {
    return left.startsWith(right + '/') || right.startsWith(left + '/');
}

const fragmentHeader = /^\s*(?:(\S+)\s*:\s*|:\s*)?\<<([^<>\r\n]+)>>=(\+)?\s*(.*?)\s*$/;
const outputFilename = /^(.*?)\s+\$(?:\s+|$)|^\$(?:\s+|$)/;

function sourceNulDiagnostic(document: SourceDocument): GenerationDiagnostic | undefined {
    const offset = document.text.indexOf('\0');
    if (offset < 0) { return undefined; }
    const lines = document.text.slice(0, offset).split(/\r\n|\r|\n/);
    return {
        path: document.path, line: lines.length - 1, column: lines[lines.length - 1].length,
        message: 'Invalid output path or source text: NUL characters are not allowed'
    };
}

```

## One document order for generation and editor maps

Fragment appends make document order significant. The root `index.literate`
comes first, followed by documents linked from it in link order, then all
remaining documents sorted by normalized path. HTML links select the matching
literate source; query strings and anchors do not affect selection. Repeated
links do not give a document another position in the order.

`orderSourceDocuments` exposes this policy to editor adapters. It returns a
new array containing the original objects and preserves all input documents.
Generation, rather than this helper, owns duplicate and invalid-path checks.
The standalone helper throws an ordinary `Error` for NUL-containing source.

```ts : <<engine document ordering>>=
/** Index first, then linked documents, then remaining paths sorted; preserves input objects. */
export function orderSourceDocuments(documents: SourceDocument[]): SourceDocument[] {
    const paths = new Map<string, number>();
    for (const document of documents) {
        const diagnostic = sourceNulDiagnostic(document);
        if (diagnostic) { throw new Error(`${diagnostic.path}: ${diagnostic.message}`); }
    }
    const canonical = (document: SourceDocument): string => safeRelativePath(document.path) ?? document.path;
    paths.set('index.literate', 0);
    const index = documents.find(document => canonical(document) === 'index.literate');
    if (index) {
        for (const token of new MarkdownIt().parse(index.text, {})) {
            for (const child of token.children || []) {
                if (child.type !== 'link_open') { continue; }
                const href = child.attrGet('href');
                if (!href) { continue; }
                let target = href.split(/[?#]/)[0];
                try { target = decodeURIComponent(target); } catch { continue; }
                const path = safeRelativePath(target.replace(/\.html$/, '.literate'));
                if (path && !paths.has(path)) { paths.set(path, paths.size); }
            }
        }
    }
    return [...documents].sort((a, b) => {
        const left = canonical(a), right = canonical(b);
        const rank = (paths.get(left) ?? paths.size) - (paths.get(right) ?? paths.size);
        return rank || (left < right ? -1 : left > right ? 1 : 0);
    });
}

```

## Discover templates without generating outputs

Adapters need to know which templates to load before they can call generation.
`templateReferences` therefore performs dependency discovery only: it reads the
root SETTINGS fence and fragment headers, returning sorted, unique, normalized
template paths. It neither expands fragments nor validates their dependency
graph. Invalid or empty template paths and NUL source text throw ordinary
`Error` objects, while missing template contents become generation diagnostics.

Output filenames are stripped before splitting fragment settings. This matters
because a filename may itself contain semicolons; they are not settings
separators until after the filename terminator.

```ts : <<engine template dependency discovery>>=
/** Discover template dependencies only. Invalid paths or NUL source text throw ordinary Errors. */
export function templateReferences(documents: SourceDocument[]): string[] {
    const references = new Set<string>();
    const md = new MarkdownIt();
    const add = (value: string): void => {
        const path = safeRelativePath(value);
        if (!path) { throw new Error(`Invalid relative path: ${value}`); }
        references.add(path);
    };
    for (const document of documents) {
        const diagnostic = sourceNulDiagnostic(document);
        if (diagnostic) { throw new Error(`${diagnostic.path}: ${diagnostic.message}`); }
        for (const token of md.parse(document.text, {})) {
            if (token.type !== 'fence') { continue; }
            if (token.info.trim() === 'SETTINGS') {
                if (safeRelativePath(document.path) === 'index.literate') {
                    for (const line of token.content.split('\n')) {
                        const setting = /^\s*template\s*=(.*)$/.exec(line);
                        if (setting) { add(setting[1].trim()); }
                    }
                }
                continue;
            }
            const header = fragmentHeader.exec(token.info);
            if (!header) { continue; }
            let settings = header[4];

            const filename = outputFilename.exec(settings);
            if (filename) { settings = settings.slice(filename[0].length); }
            for (const setting of settings.split(';').map(value => value.trim()).filter(Boolean)) {
                const template = /^template\s*=\s*(.*)$/.exec(setting);
                if (template) { add(template[1].trim()); }
            }
        }
    }
    return Array.from(references).sort();
}

```

## Templates are literal, single-pass substitutions

Source templates recognize `[CODE]`; HTML templates recognize `[CONTENT]` and
`[AUTHORS]`. A callback replaces every matching original placeholder literally.
Inserted text is never scanned again, so dollar replacement sequences and
placeholders inside generated code, author metadata, or rendered prose cannot
trigger a second substitution. Unknown placeholders remain untouched.

```ts : <<engine literal template substitution>>=
/** Replacement values are literal and are never scanned for more placeholders. */
function substitute(template: string, values: Record<string, string>): string {
    return template.replace(/\[(CODE|CONTENT|AUTHORS)\]/g, (match, key: string) =>
        Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(values, key.toLowerCase()) ? values[key.toLowerCase()] : match);
}

```

### The compatible default HTML page

Without an explicit HTML template, documentation keeps the extension's default
page structure, local stylesheet, author metadata slot, and Mermaid module.
An explicitly requested but missing template is an error, not a reason to
silently use this default.

```ts : <<engine default HTML template>>=
const defaultTemplate = `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta name="description" content="A Literate Program written with the Literate Programming vscode extension by Nathan 'jesterKing' Letwory and contributors" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="A Literate Program written with the Literate Programming vscode extension by Nathan 'jesterKing' Letwory and contributors" />
  <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="./style.css">
  [AUTHORS]
</head>
<body>
[CONTENT]
<script type="module">
  import mermaid from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mermaid@10/dist/mermaid.esm.min.mjs';
</script>
</body>
</html>`;

```

## Generation is a validation pipeline

The orchestration is deliberately split at phase boundaries rather than into
independent runtime functions. These fragments share the local scope of
`generateProject`: first establish inputs and settings, then collect fragment
definitions and reserve destinations, then validate and expand dependencies,
and only then construct output files. The validation barrier is before both
source emission and HTML rendering.

```ts : <<engine generation orchestration>>=
<<engine generation inputs and settings>>
<<engine fragment collection and output reservation>>
<<engine dependency expansion and validation barrier>>
<<engine source and HTML emission>>
```

### Establish inputs, ordering, and project settings

Input paths are normalized and duplicate source or template keys diagnosed.
NUL-containing documents never reach Markdown parsing. Generation then uses
the same ordering helper exposed to editor maps. Root SETTINGS supplies the
semicolon-separated `authors` and optional HTML `template` path; explicit
template references must resolve in the supplied template map.

```ts : <<engine generation inputs and settings>>=
/** Pure, atomic generation. Diagnostic positions are zero-based. */
export function generateProject(documents: SourceDocument[], templates: Record<string, string> = {}): {
    files: GeneratedFile[]; diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[];
} {
    const diagnostics: GenerationDiagnostic[] = [];
    const files: GeneratedFile[] = [];
    const report = (location: Location, message: string): void => {
        diagnostics.push({ ...location, message });
    };
    const md = new MarkdownIt();
    const escape = (value: string): string => md.utils.escapeHtml(value);
    const sources = new Map<string, SourceDocument>();
    const templateMap = new Map<string, string>();
    const sourceKeys = new Set<string>();
    const templateKeys = new Set<string>();
    for (const document of documents) {
        const path = safeRelativePath(document.path);
        const location = { path: document.path, line: 0, column: 0 };
        if (!path) { report(location, 'Invalid source path'); continue; }
        if (sourceKeys.has(pathKey(path))) { report(location, `Duplicate source document: ${path}`); continue; }
        sourceKeys.add(pathKey(path));
        const nul = sourceNulDiagnostic(document);
        if (nul) { diagnostics.push(nul); continue; }
        sources.set(path, { path, text: document.text });
    }
    for (const key of Object.keys(templates).sort()) {
        const path = safeRelativePath(key);
        const location = { path: key, line: 0, column: 0 };
        if (!path) { report(location, 'Invalid template path'); continue; }
        if (templateKeys.has(pathKey(path))) { report(location, `Duplicate template path: ${path}`); continue; }
        templateKeys.add(pathKey(path));
        templateMap.set(path, templates[key]);
    }
    const tokens = new Map<string, Token[]>();
    for (const document of sources.values()) { tokens.set(document.path, md.parse(document.text, {})); }
    const order = orderSourceDocuments(Array.from(sources.values())).map(document => document.path);
    const loadTemplate = (value: string, location: Location): string | undefined => {
        const path = safeRelativePath(value);
        if (!path) { report(location, `Invalid template path: ${value}`); return undefined; }
        if (!templateMap.has(path)) { report(location, `Missing template: ${path}`); return undefined; }
        return templateMap.get(path);
    };
    let authors = '';
    let htmlTemplate = defaultTemplate;
    for (const token of tokens.get('index.literate') || []) {
        if (token.type !== 'fence' || token.info.trim() !== 'SETTINGS') { continue; }
        token.content.split('\n').forEach((line: string, index: number) => {
            const match = /^\s*(authors|template)\s*=(.*)$/.exec(line);
            if (!match) { return; }
            const value = match[2].trim();
            if (match[1] === 'authors') { authors = value; }
            else {
                const loaded = loadTemplate(value, { path: 'index.literate', line: (token.map?.[0] || 0) + index + 1, column: 0 });
                if (loaded !== undefined) { htmlTemplate = loaded; }
            }
        });
    }
```

### Collect definitions and reserve every destination

Named fences use a language followed by a colon, a double-chevron name, and
`=` for a definition or `=+` for an ordered append; a language may be omitted.
Appends require an earlier definition and cannot change its output or template.
Top-level names end in `.*` and require a filename terminated by a space and
`$`, optionally followed by `template=path`.

Collection diagnoses malformed headers, empty names or filenames, duplicate
definitions, and invalid append targets. Source and HTML output paths share
one reservation set: destinations must be unique and must not overwrite any
supplied source document or template. Reserving destinations here ensures that
collisions are found before any output is returned.

```ts : <<engine fragment collection and output reservation>>=
    const fragments = new Map<string, Fragment>();
    const headers = new Map<Token, Header>();
    const sourceTemplates = new Map<string, string>();
    const outputs = new Set<string>();
    const inputKeys = new Set([...sourceKeys, ...templateKeys]);
    const reserve = (value: string, location: Location): string | undefined => {
        const path = safeRelativePath(value);
        if (!path) { report(location, `Invalid output path: ${value}`); return undefined; }
        const key = pathKey(path);
        if (inputKeys.has(key)) { report(location, `Output collides with input: ${path}`); }
        if (outputs.has(key)) { report(location, `Duplicate output path: ${path}`); }
        for (const input of inputKeys) {
            if (ancestorConflict(key, input)) { report(location, `Output/input ancestor conflict: ${path} and ${input}`); }
        }
        for (const output of outputs) {
            if (ancestorConflict(key, output)) { report(location, `Output ancestor conflict: ${path} and ${output}`); }
        }
        outputs.add(key);
        return path;
    };
    for (const path of order) {
        reserve(path.replace(/\.literate$/, '.html'), { path, line: 0, column: 0 });
        for (const token of tokens.get(path) || []) {
            if (token.type !== 'fence' || token.info.trim() === 'SETTINGS' || !token.info.includes('\<<')) { continue; }
            const location = { path, line: token.map?.[0] || 0, column: 0 };
            const match = fragmentHeader.exec(token.info);
            if (!match) { report(location, 'Invalid fragment header (expected language colon and \<<name>>= or =+)'); continue; }
            const header: Header = { language: match[1] || '', name: match[2], append: !!match[3] };
            if (!header.name.trim()) { report(location, 'Empty fragment name'); continue; }
            let remainder = match[4];
            const filename = outputFilename.exec(remainder);
            if (filename) {
                header.output = (filename[1] || '').trim();
                remainder = remainder.slice(filename[0].length);
                if (!header.output) { report(location, 'Invalid output path: empty filename'); }
            }
            for (const setting of remainder.split(';').map(value => value.trim()).filter(Boolean)) {
                const template = /^template\s*=\s*(.+)$/.exec(setting);
                if (!template || header.template !== undefined) { report(location, `Invalid fragment setting: ${setting}`); }
                else { header.template = template[1].trim(); }
            }
            headers.set(token, header);
            const part = { content: token.content, location: { ...location, line: location.line + 1 } };
            if (header.append) {
                if (header.output !== undefined || header.template !== undefined) { report(location, 'Append fragments cannot specify output or template'); }
                const fragment = fragments.get(header.name);
                if (!fragment) { report(location, `Cannot append to missing fragment: ${header.name}`); }
                else { fragment.parts.push(part); }
                continue;
            }
            if (fragments.has(header.name)) { report(location, `Duplicate fragment: ${header.name}`); continue; }
            if (header.name.endsWith('.*')) {
                if (!header.output) { report(location, `Missing filename for top-level fragment: ${header.name}`); }
                else { header.output = reserve(header.output, location); }
            } else if (header.output !== undefined || header.template !== undefined) {
                report(location, `Only top-level fragments may specify output or template: ${header.name}`);
            }
            if (header.template !== undefined) {
                const template = loadTemplate(header.template, location);
                if (template !== undefined) { sourceTemplates.set(header.name, template); }
            }
            fragments.set(header.name, { header, parts: [part], location });
        }
    }
```

### Expand dependencies while keeping literal text literal

Depth-first expansion detects missing fragments and cycles, memoizes completed
expansions, and checks even unused fragments. It has no arbitrary pass-count
cutoff. Reference diagnostics use the originating part's position, including
the reference's column within its code line.

A backslash immediately before an opening double chevron escapes it. Every
literal opening double chevron in this chapter's implementation fences is
escaped, including those inside regular expressions and strings; only the
assembly references are unescaped. Escapes survive dependency expansion and
are removed at final emission. Replacement results are never rescanned, so a
literal tag introduced by a referenced fragment cannot become a dependency.

Callbacks also preserve dollar signs and other replacement metacharacters.
Repeated inline references expand independently. Preceding whitespace is
carried onto continuation lines. Fence bodies retain their Markdown-normalized
trailing newline, while insertion removes exactly one trailing newline from a
referenced fragment. The assembly fences in this chapter use that same rule to
preserve the generated file's original whitespace and final newline exactly.

The last statement in this phase enforces the atomic-plan guarantee: any
recorded diagnostic returns an empty file list before emission can begin.

```ts : <<engine dependency expansion and validation barrier>>=
    const expanded = new Map<string, string>();
    const visiting: string[] = [];
    const expand = (name: string, location: Location): string => {
        if (expanded.has(name)) { return expanded.get(name)!; }
        const fragment = fragments.get(name);
        if (!fragment) { report(location, `Missing fragment: ${name}`); return ''; }
        if (visiting.includes(name)) { report(location, `Fragment cycle: ${visiting.concat(name).join(' -> ')}`); return ''; }
        visiting.push(name);
        const content = fragment.parts.map(part => {

            return part.content.replace(/\\\<<|\<<([^<>\r\n]+)>>(=\+?|\+)?/g,
                (whole: string, reference: string | undefined, modifier: string | undefined, offset: number) => {
                    if (reference === undefined) { return whole; }
                    const before = part.content.slice(0, offset);
                    const lineStart = before.lastIndexOf('\n') + 1;
                    const prefix = before.slice(lineStart);
                    const at = { path: part.location.path, line: part.location.line + before.split('\n').length - 1, column: prefix.length };
                    if (modifier) { report(at, `Invalid definition operator on reference: ${reference}`); }
                    const replacement = expand(reference, at).replace(/\n$/, '');
                    const indent = /^[ \t]*$/.test(prefix) ? prefix : (/[ \t]*$/.exec(prefix)?.[0] || '');
                    return replacement.replace(/\n/g, () => '\n' + indent);
                });
        }).join('');
        visiting.pop();
        expanded.set(name, content);
        return content;
    };

    for (const fragment of fragments.values()) { expand(fragment.header.name, fragment.location); }
    if (diagnostics.length) { return { files: [], diagnostics }; }
```

### Emit sources and render documentation only after validation

Successful source expansion is unescaped once, then inserted into its source
template. Template text itself is not treated as fragment source. The engine
builds an in-memory file list; it never performs filesystem writes.

Documentation hides SETTINGS fences and highlighted comments in named code
fences without removing comments from generated source. Named fences retain
the `codefragment`, `fragmentname`, `code`, and `literate-tag-name` styling hooks.
Labels and author metadata are HTML escaped. Mermaid receives escaped raw code
rather than syntax-highlighted markup. Finally the HTML template receives the
rendered content and author metadata in one literal substitution pass. Before
returning, every final source and HTML file is normalized to LF line endings,
including text supplied by templates. This produces deterministic bytes across
hosts without adding or removing a final newline.

```ts : <<engine source and HTML emission>>=
    for (const fragment of fragments.values()) {
        if (!fragment.header.output) { continue; }
        const code = expanded.get(fragment.header.name)!.replace(/\\\<</g, () => '\<<');
        files.push({ path: fragment.header.output, content: substitute(sourceTemplates.get(fragment.header.name) ?? '[CODE]', { code }) });
    }
    md.renderer.rules.fence = (fenceTokens: Token[], index: number) => {
        const token = fenceTokens[index];
        if (token.info.trim() === 'SETTINGS') { return ''; }
        const header = headers.get(token);
        const language = header?.language || token.info.trim().split(/\s+/)[0];
        const code = token.content.replace(/\\\<</g, () => '\<<');
        if (!header && language === 'mermaid') { return `<pre class="mermaid">\n${escape(code)}</pre>\n`; }
        let rendered = language && hljs.getLanguage(language)
            ? hljs.highlight(code, { language }).value : escape(code);
        if (header) {
            rendered = rendered.replace(/<span class="hljs-comment">.*?<\/span>/gs, () => '');
            rendered = rendered.replace(/&lt;&lt;.*?&gt;&gt;/g, (tag: string) =>
                `<span class="literate-tag-name">${tag.replace(/<\/?span\b[^>]*>/g, () => '')}</span>`);
        }
        const block = `<pre><code>${rendered}</code></pre>\n`;
        if (!header) { return block; }
        const label = escape(`\<<${header.name}>>=${header.append ? '+' : ''} ${header.output ? header.output + ' $' : ''}`);
        return `<div class="codefragment">\n<div class="fragmentname">${label}</div>\n<div class="code">\n${block}\n</div>\n</div>`;
    };
    const metaAuthors = authors.split(';').map(author => `<meta name="author" content="${escape(author)}">`).join('');
    for (const path of order) {
        const content = md.renderer.render(tokens.get(path)!, md.options, {});
        files.push({ path: safeRelativePath(path.replace(/\.literate$/, '.html'))!, content: substitute(htmlTemplate, { content, authors: metaAuthors }) });
    }
    for (const file of files) { file.content = file.content.replace(/\r\n|\r/g, '\n'); }
    return { files, diagnostics };
}
```
